$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Equipment. A schematic overview of the microreaction system is shown in
Figure 6. In the experiment, TFA and COX which was dissolved in
acetonitrile were both delivered by metering pumps (Beijing Satellite Co.
Ltd.). The mixing was performed in a microvolume T-shaped mixer (Beijing
Mingnike Analytical Instrument Center). The micromixer is made of PTFE
(polytetrafluoroethylene) material with an inner diameter of 0.25 mm. The
reactants were pumped through the pipes immersed in an oil bath to
control the feeding temperature. A delay loop (PTFE) immersed in the oil
bath with an inner diameter of 2 mm and an external diameter of 3 mm was
connected directly downstream to the Micromixer 1 to control the
residence time. The residence time could be controlled accurately by
changing the length of the delay loop or the flow velocity. The pressure
was controlled at 0.6 MPa by a metering valve (Beijing Xiongchuan
Technology Ltd.) at the outlet right after the Micromixer 2. During the
experiments, it was found that nearly no vaporization occurred in the
microsystem even at the high temperature of 130 °C. In order to control
residence time accurately, Micromixer 2 was designed to quench the
reaction by adding a large amount of acetonitrile to the reacting system.
Ronchin et al.14 has described the kinetics of the rearrangement by TFA
in acetonitrile at 90 °C. The conversion of COX reached only 15% with the
reaction time of 50 min and TFA concentration of 1 mol/L. Thus, we can
figure out that the reaction in the mixtures (TFA concentration <1 mol/L)
is stopped after the dilution of acetonitrile at room temperature. During
the experiments, the acetonitrile solution and TFA were pumped into
Micromixer 1 to induce the rearrangement reaction and then reaction went
on in the delay loop. A large amount of acetonitrile was pumped into
Micromixer 2 to quench the reaction by both cooling and dilution of
reaction mixture. After about 5 min, the system was stable, and then the
samples were collected directly at the outlet. The sampling time was also
recorded with a stopwatch.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM microreaction system used, Beckmann rearrangement, alternatively batch
reactor system may be used
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Organocatalyzed Beckmann Rearrangement of Cyclohexanone Oxime in a
Microchemical System
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Zhang, Jisong; Dong, Chen; Du, Chencan; Luo, Guangsheng
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Process Research & Development (2015), 19(2), 352-356

